1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:05,050
Before HDB: The Singapore
Improvement Trust and Tiong Bahru

2
00:00:05,050 --> 00:00:09,206
Walk around Tiong Bahru today
and the rounded corners,

3
00:00:09,286 --> 00:00:12,893
curved balconies and
porthole windows read as a

4
00:00:12,973 --> 00:00:16,973
heritage flex — a well-preserved
pocket of 1930s Art

5
00:00:17,053 --> 00:00:20,975
Deco that estate agents
now market as a lifestyle.

6
00:00:20,975 --> 00:00:24,425
Almost none of it was
built to be charming.

7
00:00:24,425 --> 00:00:28,893
It was Singapore's first public
housing, built by a colonial body

8
00:00:28,973 --> 00:00:34,211
that no longer exists, to get people
out of exactly the kind of overcrowded,

9
00:00:34,291 --> 00:00:38,350
disease-ridden slum that Tiong
Bahru itself had once been.

10
00:00:38,350 --> 00:00:42,086
By the 1910s and 20s, the
ground that would become

11
00:00:42,166 --> 00:00:45,979
Tiong Bahru was a byword
for exactly the conditions

12
00:00:46,059 --> 00:00:49,875
colonial Singapore was
trying to be seen tackling.

13
00:00:49,875 --> 00:00:52,804
The area sat close to
the General Hospital,

14
00:00:52,884 --> 00:00:57,283
which made its state a particular
embarrassment: mangrove swamp,

15
00:00:57,363 --> 00:01:01,272
poor drainage, and roads the
Singapore Free Press in 1914

16
00:01:01,352 --> 00:01:05,611
described as "dilapidated and
dangerous" and "in a disgraceful

17
00:01:05,691 --> 00:01:09,400
state of neglect, being full
of huge holes and ruts."

18
00:01:09,400 --> 00:01:14,075
It bred mosquitoes and disease
on a hospital's doorstep.

19
00:01:14,075 --> 00:01:19,827
Further off in Chinatown, the same
overcrowding was worse and better documented:

20
00:01:19,907 --> 00:01:22,597
a 1907 public health report and a 1918

21
00:01:22,677 --> 00:01:26,970
housing commission had already
argued that Singapore's slums

22
00:01:27,050 --> 00:01:30,469
needed more than road tax
and ad hoc clearance —

23
00:01:30,549 --> 00:01:35,725
they needed a dedicated body with
the power to plan, build and rehouse.

24
00:01:35,725 --> 00:01:40,623
The municipal health officer P. S.
Hunter's own study of Chinatown's

25
00:01:40,703 --> 00:01:44,875
congestion made the same case
directly to the government.

26
00:01:44,875 --> 00:01:48,114
That body was the Singapore
Improvement Trust,

27
00:01:48,194 --> 00:01:53,742
established on the 1st of July 1927 under
the Singapore Improvement Ordinance,

28
00:01:53,822 --> 00:01:55,834
empowered "to provide for the

29
00:01:55,914 --> 00:01:59,450
Improvement of the Town
and Island of Singapore."

30
00:01:59,450 --> 00:02:02,675
Tiong Bahru was its proving ground.

31
00:02:02,675 --> 00:02:06,347
A clearance scheme began in 1925, land —

32
00:02:06,427 --> 00:02:12,914
33 hectares of it — was formally
acquired in 1926 for around $600,000,

33
00:02:12,994 --> 00:02:16,666
and basic infrastructure (roads, drains,

34
00:02:16,746 --> 00:02:22,750
a canal) took until 1931 to
complete, at a further $1.5 million.

35
00:02:22,750 --> 00:02:26,308
Only then did the Trust
start building flats:

36
00:02:26,388 --> 00:02:31,564
construction began in March 1936,
and the first completed block —

37
00:02:31,644 --> 00:02:35,525
28 units over four shops
— opened that December.

38
00:02:35,525 --> 00:02:39,144
By 1941 the Trust had put up 784

39
00:02:39,224 --> 00:02:43,500
flats housing more than 6,000 people.

40
00:02:43,500 --> 00:02:47,672
The architects — Lincoln Page,
Robert F. N. Kan and A. G. Church —

41
00:02:47,752 --> 00:02:50,699
borrowed from British
new towns like Stevenage,

42
00:02:50,779 --> 00:02:54,564
Harlow and Crawley, but built
in a Streamline Moderne style:

43
00:02:54,644 --> 00:02:58,494
rounded corners, sweeping
horizontal lines, porthole windows,

44
00:02:58,574 --> 00:03:02,488
stainless-steel railings, spiral
staircases, and prewar blocks

45
00:03:02,568 --> 00:03:06,175
that folded in shophouse-style
courtyards and air-wells.

46
00:03:06,175 --> 00:03:09,783
Locals gave the earliest blocks
a nickname of their own —

47
00:03:09,863 --> 00:03:13,405
puay-kee chu, "aeroplane
houses" — because their curved,

48
00:03:13,485 --> 00:03:17,934
tower-like massing reminded people
of Kallang Airport's control tower,

49
00:03:18,014 --> 00:03:22,025
finished around the same time
on the other side of the island.

50
00:03:22,025 --> 00:03:25,869
Tiong Bahru's flats were a step
up from a shophouse cubicle,

51
00:03:25,949 --> 00:03:29,073
and the people who could
afford that step up were

52
00:03:29,153 --> 00:03:32,750
not always who a government
housing scheme had in mind.

53
00:03:32,750 --> 00:03:37,011
The estate's Chinese nickname,
mei-ren wo — "den of beauties" —

54
00:03:37,091 --> 00:03:41,628
came from its reputation for housing
the mistresses of wealthy men,

55
00:03:41,708 --> 00:03:45,142
along with cabaret singers
and dance hostesses from

56
00:03:45,222 --> 00:03:49,425
the nightclub scene around Great
World Amusement Park nearby.

57
00:03:49,425 --> 00:03:52,957
Locals had a third name
for it, only half joking:

58
00:03:53,037 --> 00:03:55,175
the "Hollywood of Singapore."

59
00:03:55,175 --> 00:03:58,642
The flats themselves didn't
discriminate — Chinese,

60
00:03:58,722 --> 00:04:02,537
Indian, Eurasian and European
families lived there too —

61
00:04:02,617 --> 00:04:06,025
but the nickname stuck to
the estate for decades.

62
00:04:06,025 --> 00:04:10,044
The Japanese Occupation damaged
rooftops across the estate

63
00:04:10,124 --> 00:04:14,921
and stalled further building, though
it didn't stop people moving in:

64
00:04:15,001 --> 00:04:18,525
close to 40% of Tiong
Bahru's postwar residents had

65
00:04:18,605 --> 00:04:22,775
actually taken up their flats
during the Occupation itself.

66
00:04:22,775 --> 00:04:27,103
The community that formed
there kept growing afterward —

67
00:04:27,183 --> 00:04:30,488
the first residents'
club appeared in 1948,

68
00:04:30,568 --> 00:04:36,000
and Singapore's first community
centre opened in Tiong Bahru in 1951.

69
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:40,225
But the Trust that had built
it all was on borrowed time.

70
00:04:40,225 --> 00:04:44,016
The Housing and Development
Ordinance took effect on the

71
00:04:44,096 --> 00:04:47,817
1st of February 1960, formally
dissolving the Singapore

72
00:04:47,897 --> 00:04:51,549
Improvement Trust and handing
its housing mandate to a

73
00:04:51,629 --> 00:04:55,500
new statutory board — the
Housing and Development Board.

74
00:04:55,500 --> 00:05:00,042
By then the Trust had also
drawn up Singapore's 1952 to

75
00:05:00,122 --> 00:05:04,664
1958 Master Plan, the zoning
and new-town framework HDB

76
00:05:04,744 --> 00:05:09,450
would build its much larger
housing programme on top of.

77
00:05:09,450 --> 00:05:12,905
Tiong Bahru wasn't just
SIT's biggest project;

78
00:05:12,985 --> 00:05:16,750
it was the prototype for
everything HDB did next.

79
00:05:16,750 --> 00:05:19,650
For a while, Tiong Bahru just aged.

80
00:05:19,650 --> 00:05:23,940
Rental flats were converted
to sales in the 1960s and 70s,

81
00:05:24,020 --> 00:05:28,763
the last of the surrounding slums
were cleared by the mid-1970s,

82
00:05:28,843 --> 00:05:32,455
and by the 1980s and 90s
the estate had an ageing

83
00:05:32,535 --> 00:05:36,297
population and little else
to recommend it — small,

84
00:05:36,377 --> 00:05:41,275
dated flats with none of the newer
HDB towns' scale or amenities.

85
00:05:41,275 --> 00:05:43,995
What reversed that was the opposite of

86
00:05:44,075 --> 00:05:46,875
what built it: not need, but heritage.

87
00:05:46,875 --> 00:05:51,198
Twenty prewar blocks were given
conservation status in 2003,

88
00:05:51,278 --> 00:05:55,895
and from the early 2000s young
professionals, gallery owners and

89
00:05:55,975 --> 00:06:00,151
boutique operators moved in,
drawn by exactly the Art Deco

90
00:06:00,231 --> 00:06:05,075
character the original tenants had
never been asked to appreciate.

91
00:06:05,075 --> 00:06:08,100
Property prices climbed with them.

92
00:06:08,100 --> 00:06:12,168
Conservation flats that once
housed dockworkers and cabaret

93
00:06:12,248 --> 00:06:16,035
girls now command some of
the highest resale and rental

94
00:06:16,115 --> 00:06:19,058
prices of any HDB-era
housing in Singapore,

95
00:06:19,138 --> 00:06:23,698
some converted into boutique hotels
— despite carrying some of the

96
00:06:23,778 --> 00:06:27,575
shortest remaining leases
in the public housing stock.

97
00:06:27,575 --> 00:06:32,981
The prewar blocks were privatised
on 99-year leases dated

98
00:06:33,061 --> 00:06:37,505
from the 1st of January
1967, expiring in 2066;

99
00:06:37,585 --> 00:06:43,376
the postwar blocks that make up
most of the 2003 conservation

100
00:06:43,456 --> 00:06:48,750
area run on 99-year leases
from 1973, expiring in 2072.

101
00:06:48,750 --> 00:06:52,694
Conservation status forecloses
the usual way an ageing

102
00:06:52,774 --> 00:06:56,717
HDB estate resets that clock
— en-bloc redevelopment —

103
00:06:56,797 --> 00:07:00,368
so short of a government
buyback and fresh lease,

104
00:07:00,448 --> 00:07:04,025
Tiong Bahru's remaining
lease only gets shorter.

105
00:07:04,025 --> 00:07:07,531
In a market where remaining
lease is normally one of

106
00:07:07,611 --> 00:07:12,082
the strongest drags on price, an
estate this far into a shrinking,

107
00:07:12,162 --> 00:07:14,771
non-resettable lease still commanding a

108
00:07:14,851 --> 00:07:18,575
premium is a genuine exception,
not just a high price.

109
00:07:18,575 --> 00:07:22,647
The estate built to solve a slum
problem is today priced well

110
00:07:22,727 --> 00:07:26,675
out of reach of the kind of
household it was designed for.

111
00:07:26,675 --> 00:07:30,771
Why it matters today:
Singapore's public housing

112
00:07:30,851 --> 00:07:35,550
story is usually told starting
with HDB and the 1960s.

113
00:07:35,550 --> 00:07:39,770
Tiong Bahru is the reminder
that the model — clear the slum,

114
00:07:39,850 --> 00:07:44,357
build a planned estate, hand it
to the people who need it most —

115
00:07:44,437 --> 00:07:48,227
was already three decades
old by the time HDB existed,

116
00:07:48,307 --> 00:07:52,750
tested and refined by a colonial
trust nobody remembers today.

117
00:07:52,750 --> 00:07:55,836
The estate's second life,
as heritage property for

118
00:07:55,916 --> 00:07:58,622
people who don't need
public housing at all,

119
00:07:58,702 --> 00:08:02,375
is a different story the SIT
could never have planned for.
